{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-129401",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(1,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (1,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (1,0)$ along the path $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0)\\,\\,\\,x=1}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,0)}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}=\\text{DNE}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
